\section{Motivation for Non-Commutable Quantum Operators}
Classical quantum mechanics assumes unitary evolution governed by a Hermitian
Hamiltonian $\hat{H}$. However, gravitational collapse generates infinite-recursion
microstates that cannot be represented within a Turing-computable Hilbert space.
Let $\mathcal{H}_{\text{bounded}}$ denote the standard Hilbert space of finite-energy
states. Collapse dynamics produce states requiring:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{\text{non-Turing}} \neq \emptyset,
\end{equation}
necessitating an oracle-extended Hilbert space.

\section{Definition of the Oracle-Extended Hilbert Space}
SASA-v4.0 defines:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{\text{oracle}} =
\mathcal{H}_{\text{bounded}} \otimes
\mathcal{H}_{\text{non-Turing}},
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{H}_{\text{non-Turing}}$ contains states encoding non-computable
recursion limits. The non-unitary operator $\hat{\Omega}_{\text{uncomp}}$ acts on
$\mathcal{H}_{\text{oracle}}$ and possesses a complex eigenvalue spectrum:
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{Spec}(\hat{\Omega}_{\text{uncomp}}) = \{\omega_m\}.
\end{equation}

\section{Eigenvalue Sector Classification}
The eigenvalues fall into three sectors:
\begin{align}
|\omega_m| &= 1 \quad \text{(unitary sector)}, \\
0 < |\omega_m| < 1 \quad \text{(damped firewall sector)}, \\
\omega_m &= 0 \quad \text{(oracle null sector)}.
\end{align}
The unitary sector recovers standard QFT in low-curvature regions. The damped
firewall sector absorbs trans-Planckian divergences. The oracle null sector traps
infinite-recursion microstates.

